-- A MEASURED NEGATIVE RESULT: a shape whose answer IS decided by `impl`-BLOCK order
-- is INVARIANT under `interface`-declaration order. The two axes are independent.
--
-- ⭐ WHAT THIS REPLACED, AND WHY THE ANCHOR MOVED FROM #1182 TO #1183. Until Q1
-- (Stage B / Phase 4b) this slot held negative-1182-impl-axis-shape-is-iface-axis-
-- invariant/, which carried #1182's declarations byte-for-byte: TWO interfaces both
-- declaring `m`, whose impls the selector ranked against each other. Q1 rejects that
-- file ON THE DECLARATION, so the proposition needed a different impl-order-sensitive
-- carrier. #1183 is one, and it is still live: `Sh`'s two impls here are
-- ⊑-incomparable and the goal is NOT closed (`[]` leaves an unbound metavariable), so
-- F-3d's coherence reject is deferred and `pickMostSpecificEntry` commits to the HEAD
-- of the candidate list -- the value is decided by which `impl Sh` block is written
-- first. This is test/dict_fixtures/s6-2-t4-open-goal-deferred.mdk's shape, carried in
-- test/diff_compiler_dict_semantics.sh §4 as a KNOWN-BAD permutation row against
-- #1183, plus a second interface so this gate's permuter has two chunks to move.
--
-- ⚠️ THE #1182 SHAPE IS NOT MERELY MOVED, IT IS GONE FROM THIS CORPUS. Its
-- impl-order sensitivity came from a candidate set SPANNING TWO INTERFACES that
-- shared a method name; Q1 makes that unrepresentable within one module. Within ONE
-- interface, overlapping impls at a CLOSED goal are rejected outright as
-- `Ambiguous instance` (measured on this branch), which is why the carrier had to be
-- the OPEN-goal #1183 shape and not a closed one.
--
-- ⚠️ NOT AN ASSERTION THAT 1 IS RIGHT. Same disclaimer the replaced file carried:
-- §6.2 T4's actual answer is to DEFER a non-closed goal to quiescence, which this
-- compiler has no pass for. What is asserted is only that the two interface-
-- declaration orderings AGREE WITH EACH OTHER.
--
-- Unledgered on purpose, so the gate grades it as invariant and reds if it moves: if
-- a later Stage B unit makes THIS shape start depending on interface order, the fix
-- has introduced an order dependence where there was none. Do not ledger it to make
-- that green.
data Q a b c = Q a b c

interface Sh a where
  sh : a -> Int

interface Tg a where
  tg : a -> Int

impl Sh (Q Int y z) where
  sh _ = 1

impl Sh (Q x Bool z) where
  sh _ = 2

impl Tg (Q Int y z) where
  tg _ = 9

main = println (sh (Q 1 True []))
